<script>
	import Admonition from '#site/components/Admonition.svelte';
	import RotateCoordinatesExample from '#site/demos/examples/RotateCoordinatesExample.svelte';
</script>

# Cropper

`Cropper` is the default cropper component: a resizable, movable stencil over an image that can be panned, zoomed and rotated. It assembles the replaceable parts (wrapper, boundary, background, stencil) and exposes the full imperative API. For a stencil with a fixed on-screen size, use [`FixedCropper`](/docs/components/FixedCropper) instead.

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
	import { Cropper, type CropperRef } from 'svelte-advanced-cropper';
	import 'svelte-advanced-cropper/style.css';

	let cropper: CropperRef | undefined = $state();

	function onChange(cropper: CropperRef) {
		console.log(cropper.getCoordinates());
	}
</script>

<Cropper bind:this={cropper} src="/images/photo.jpg" {onChange} />
<button onclick={() => cropper?.rotateImage(90)}>Rotate</button>
```

The page lists everything the component accepts. Settings such as `aspectRatio` or `minWidth` are ordinary props: `Cropper` sorts them into core settings for you.

## Props

### `src`

**Type:** `string | null`

The URL of the image to crop. Data URLs and blob URLs (for example from `URL.createObjectURL`) work too. When it changes, the cropper loads the new image and resets its state. Setting it to `null` clears the cropper.

### `class`

**Type:** `ClassValue`

Classes for the root element of the cropper, which also carries `advanced-cropper`. It takes anything Svelte's `class` attribute takes: a string, an array or an object. This is upstream's `className`.

### `style`

**Type:** `string`

An inline style string for the root element.

### `stencilComponent`

**Type:** `Component` · **Default:** `RectangleStencil`

The component that draws the stencil. Pass [`CircleStencil`](/docs/components/CircleStencil) for a round crop, or your own component. A custom stencil receives `cropper`, `image` and `disabled` along with everything in `stencilProps`. Its exports (read through `bind:this`) are passed to `stencilConstraints`, which is how a stencil can impose an aspect ratio. See [Change the stencil](/docs/guides/recipes#change-the-stencil) and the [custom stencil tutorial](/docs/tutorials/custom-stencil).

### `stencilProps`

**Type:** `Record<string, any>` · **Default:** `{}`

Props for the stencil component, for example `aspectRatio`, `grid` or `handlers`. The cropper also hands this object to `stencilConstraints`, so stencil options such as `aspectRatio` take effect even before the stencil has mounted. See the [`RectangleStencil` props](/docs/components/RectangleStencil#props).

### `stencilConstraints`

**Type:** `(settings, stencilOptions) => Partial<Settings>` · **Default:** `defaultStencilConstraints`

Turns stencil options into cropper settings. `stencilOptions` merges `stencilProps` with the stencil component's exports (the exports win), and the result is spread over the settings. The default function derives `aspectRatio` from the stencil, unless you set the `aspectRatio` prop on the cropper yourself. Stencil options may be values or functions, so a constraints function should accept both.

### `disabled`

**Type:** `boolean` · **Default:** `false`

Turns off every interaction: the stencil can't be moved or resized, and the image can't be dragged, zoomed or rotated. A disabled cropper also stops capturing wheel and touch events, so the page can scroll over it.

### `canvas`

**Type:** `boolean` · **Default:** `true`

Renders the hidden [`CropperCanvas`](/docs/components/CropperCanvas) that `getCanvas()` draws into. Set it to `false` if you never need the cropped image as a canvas, and `getCanvas()` will return `null`.

### `crossOrigin`

**Type:** `'anonymous' | 'use-credentials' | boolean` · **Default:** `true`

The CORS mode used to load the image and set on the background `<img>`. `true` means `'anonymous'`, and `false` leaves the attribute off. Drawing a cross-origin image onto a canvas needs CORS, so `getCanvas()` relies on it. See [Cross-Origin](/docs/guides/cross-origin).

### `checkOrientation`

**Type:** `boolean` · **Default:** `true`

Reads the EXIF orientation of the image and applies it, so photos taken on phones appear upright.

### `unloadTime`

**Type:** `number` · **Default:** `500`

A delay in milliseconds that serves two purposes:

1. When the image is cleared, `isLoaded()` turns `false` straight away, but the image itself is removed only after this delay.
2. When one image replaces another, loading takes at least this long.

Both give a custom [`wrapperComponent`](/docs/components/CropperWrapper) time to run a fade-out transition before the image changes.

### `autoReconcileState`

**Type:** `boolean` · **Default:** `true`

Whenever the state stops satisfying the settings (for example after you change `minWidth`) and no interaction is running, the cropper repairs it with `reconcileState()`. Set to `false` to call `reconcileState()` yourself. This is read once, when the cropper mounts.

### `transitions`

**Type:** `boolean | CropperTransitionsSettings` · **Default:** `true`

```ts
interface CropperTransitionsSettings {
	timingFunction?: string; // default 'ease-in-out'
	duration?: number; // in milliseconds, default 350
}
```

Animates changes that aren't driven by a pointer: auto-zoom after a resize, rotating and flipping, and the methods that accept a `transitions` option. Pass `false` to apply such changes instantly.

### `wrapperComponent`

**Type:** `Component` · **Default:** `CropperWrapper`

The outermost component. It receives `cropper`, `class`, `style`, `disabled` and a `children` snippet, and must render the snippet and apply `class` and `style` to its root element. See [`CropperWrapper`](/docs/components/CropperWrapper).

### `wrapperProps`

**Type:** `Record<string, any>` · **Default:** `{}`

Extra props for `wrapperComponent`. They are spread first, so they can't override `cropper`, `class`, `style` or `disabled`.

### `backgroundWrapperComponent`

**Type:** `Component` · **Default:** `CropperBackgroundWrapper`

The component around the image and the stencil that turns gestures (drag, wheel, pinch) into image transforms. See [`CropperBackgroundWrapper`](/docs/components/CropperBackgroundWrapper).

### `backgroundWrapperProps`

**Type:** `Record<string, any>` · **Default:** `{}`

Extra props for `backgroundWrapperComponent`. With the default component, this is where you switch gestures on or off:

```svelte
<Cropper {src} backgroundWrapperProps={{ scaleImage: false, rotateImage: true }} />
```

### `backgroundWrapperClassName`

**Type:** `ClassValue`

Classes for the background wrapper element, next to `advanced-cropper__background-wrapper`.

<Admonition type="info" title="Differs from React">

react-advanced-cropper declares this prop but never applies it. Here it works.

</Admonition>

### `backgroundComponent`

**Type:** `Component` · **Default:** `CropperBackgroundImage`

The component that shows the transformed image behind the stencil. It must declare a bindable `ref` prop and bind it to the element it draws (an `<img>` or a `<canvas>`), because `getCanvas()` uses that element as the source. See [`CropperBackgroundImage`](/docs/components/CropperBackgroundImage).

### `backgroundProps`

**Type:** `Record<string, any>` · **Default:** `{}`

Extra props for `backgroundComponent`.

### `backgroundClassName`

**Type:** `ClassValue`

Classes for the background image, next to `advanced-cropper__background`.

### `boundaryComponent`

**Type:** `Component` · **Default:** `StretchableBoundary`

The component that sizes the working area and reports that size to the cropper. A replacement must export `stretchTo(size)` and `reset()`. See [`StretchableBoundary`](/docs/components/StretchableBoundary).

### `boundaryProps`

**Type:** `Record<string, any>`

Extra props for `boundaryComponent`. With the default boundary you can pass a custom `stretchAlgorithm` or `sizeAlgorithm` here:

```svelte
<Cropper {src} boundaryProps={{ stretchAlgorithm: myStretchAlgorithm }} />
```

### `boundaryClassName`

**Type:** `ClassValue`

Classes for the boundary element, next to `advanced-cropper__boundary`. You rarely need it.

### `postProcess`

**Type:** `PostprocessFunction | PostprocessFunction[]`

One or more functions that can adjust the state after each cropper action (creating the state, transforming the image, moving or resizing the stencil, ending an interaction, …). Each receives the new state, the settings and a description of the action, and returns the state to use. With an array, they run in order. `FixedCropper` uses one to keep the stencil size fixed.

```ts
type PostprocessFunction = (
	state: CropperState,
	settings: Settings,
	action: PostprocessAction
) => CropperState;
```

## Settings

Settings configure the core state algorithms. They are plain props on `Cropper`, and any of them can change at any time: the state is reconciled with the new values.

### `imageRestriction`

**Type:** `'fitArea' | 'fillArea' | 'stencil' | 'none'` (the `ImageRestriction` enum) · **Default:** `'fitArea'`

Limits how far the image can be moved and zoomed:

- `fitArea`: the image may not be zoomed out further than fits the area, and it is kept inside the area as far as possible.
- `fillArea`: the image always fills the whole area, so no empty space shows around it.
- `stencil`: the image always covers the stencil. Pair it with `FixedCropper`.
- `none`: no limits at all.

See [Different image restrictions](/docs/guides/advanced-recipes#different-image-restrictions).

### `minWidth`

**Type:** `number`

The minimum width of the crop area, in image pixels.

### `minHeight`

**Type:** `number`

The minimum height of the crop area, in image pixels.

### `maxWidth`

**Type:** `number`

The maximum width of the crop area, in image pixels.

### `maxHeight`

**Type:** `number`

The maximum height of the crop area, in image pixels.

### `aspectRatio`

**Type:** `number | { minimum?: number; maximum?: number } | ((state, settings) => RawAspectRatio)`

The aspect ratio (width divided by height) of the crop area, either fixed or as a range. Setting it here overrides the stencil's own aspect ratio, so you would normally pass it through `stencilProps` instead (see [Set the aspect ratio](/docs/guides/recipes#set-the-aspect-ratio)).

### `priority`

**Type:** `'coordinates' | 'visibleArea'` (the `Priority` enum) · **Default:** `'coordinates'`

Which default is computed first when a new state is created:

- `'coordinates'`: the crop area is computed first. `defaultSize` and `defaultPosition` don't know the visible area yet.
- `'visibleArea'`: the visible area is computed first. `defaultVisibleArea` doesn't know the coordinates yet.

<Admonition type="tip" title="Rule of thumb">

Use `'coordinates'` when you set default coordinates, and `'visibleArea'` when you set a default visible area.

</Admonition>

### `defaultCoordinates`

**Type:** `CoordinatesTransform | CoordinatesTransform[] | ((state, settings) => CoordinatesTransform | CoordinatesTransform[])`

The initial crop area. It accepts everything `setCoordinates()` accepts. When set, it takes precedence over `defaultSize` and `defaultPosition`.

```ts
const defaultCoordinates = { left: 100, top: 50, width: 400, height: 300 };
```

See [Default coordinates](/docs/guides/advanced-recipes#default-coordinates).

### `defaultPosition`

**Type:** `Position | ((state: CropperState, settings) => Position)`

The initial position of the crop area, in image pixels. Pass an object such as `{ left: 142, top: 73 }`, or a function of the state and settings that returns one. This centers the crop area:

```ts
const defaultPosition = ({ imageSize, coordinates }: CropperState) => ({
	left: imageSize.width / 2 - coordinates.width / 2,
	top: imageSize.height / 2 - coordinates.height / 2
});
```

See [Position / Size](/docs/guides/advanced-recipes#position-size).

### `defaultSize`

**Type:** `Size | ((state: CropperState, settings) => Size)`

The initial size of the crop area, in image pixels. Pass an object such as `{ width: 142, height: 73 }`, or a function of the state and settings. This one fills the visible area:

```ts
const defaultSize = ({ visibleArea, imageSize }: CropperState) => ({
	width: (visibleArea || imageSize).width,
	height: (visibleArea || imageSize).height
});
```

### `defaultVisibleArea`

**Type:** `VisibleArea | ((state: CropperState, settings) => VisibleArea)`

The part of the image that is initially visible, as `{ left, top, width, height }` in image pixels, or a function of the state and settings that returns it.

<Admonition type="warning" title="Remember">

The visible area always has the boundary's aspect ratio. If yours doesn't, the cropper changes its height to match.

</Admonition>

```ts
const defaultVisibleArea = ({ coordinates }: CropperState) => ({
	left: coordinates.left - 50,
	top: coordinates.top - 50,
	width: coordinates.width + 100,
	height: coordinates.height + 100
});
```

See [Default visible area](/docs/guides/advanced-recipes#default-visible-area).

### `defaultTransforms`

**Type:** `PartialTransforms | ((image: CropperImage) => PartialTransforms)`

```ts
interface PartialTransforms {
	rotate?: number;
	flip?: {
		horizontal?: boolean;
		vertical?: boolean;
	};
}
```

The rotation and flip applied when an image loads, as an object or as a function of the loaded image (whose `transforms` already include the EXIF orientation). For example, to rotate every image by a further 90 degrees:

```ts
const defaultTransforms = (image: CropperImage) => ({
	...image.transforms,
	rotate: image.transforms.rotate + 90
});
```

See [Default transforms](/docs/guides/advanced-recipes#default-transforms).

### `transformImage`

**Type:** `{ adjustStencil?: boolean }` · **Default:** `{ adjustStencil: true }`

Settings for image transforms. With `adjustStencil`, the stencil shrinks along with the image when the user zooms in, which helps when size restrictions would otherwise block the zoom. Turn it off for a fixed stencil, because it changes the stencil's size (`FixedCropper` always turns it off). See [Adjust stencil](/docs/guides/advanced-recipes#adjust-stencil).

### `moveCoordinates`

**Type:** `object`

Settings for the move-coordinates algorithm. The default algorithm has none; it exists for custom algorithms and extensions.

### `resizeCoordinates`

**Type:** `object`

Settings for the resize-coordinates algorithm. As with `moveCoordinates`, the default algorithm reads nothing from it.

### `sizeRestrictions`

**Type:** `SizeRestrictions | ((state: CropperState, settings) => SizeRestrictions)` · **Default:** derived from `minWidth`, `maxWidth`, `minHeight` and `maxHeight`

```ts
interface SizeRestrictions {
	minWidth: number;
	maxWidth: number;
	minHeight: number;
	maxHeight: number;
}
```

The size limits of the crop area. The default combines the four min/max props with the image size. Pass a function to compute limits from the state, for example relative to the image size. See [Custom size restrictions](/docs/guides/advanced-recipes#custom-size-restrictions).

### `positionRestrictions`

**Type:** `PositionRestrictions | ((state: CropperState, settings) => PositionRestrictions)`

```ts
interface PositionRestrictions {
	left?: number;
	top?: number;
	right?: number;
	bottom?: number;
}
```

Limits for where the crop area can go. By default the image restriction decides this.

### `areaSizeRestrictions`

**Type:** `SizeRestrictions | ((state: CropperState, settings) => SizeRestrictions)`

Size limits for the visible area, which control how far the image can be zoomed in or out.

### `areaPositionRestrictions`

**Type:** `PositionRestrictions | ((state: CropperState, settings) => PositionRestrictions)`

Position limits for the visible area, which control how far the image can be moved.

## Algorithms

These props replace the core algorithms that compute new states. You only need them for unusual behaviour. Each receives the current state and the settings, and returns a new state.

### `createStateAlgorithm`

**Type:** `(options: CreateStateOptions, settings) => CropperState`

```ts
interface CreateStateOptions {
	boundary: Boundary;
	image: Size & { transforms?: Transforms };
}
```

Creates the initial state for an image and a boundary.

### `reconcileStateAlgorithm`

**Type:** `(state: CropperState, settings) => CropperState`

Brings a state back in line with the settings. It runs when the settings change.

### `setCoordinatesAlgorithm`

**Type:** `(state: CropperState, settings, transforms: CoordinatesTransform | CoordinatesTransform[], safe?: boolean | SetCoordinatesMode) => CropperState`

Used by `setCoordinates()`.

### `setVisibleAreaAlgorithm`

**Type:** `(state: CropperState, settings, visibleArea: VisibleArea) => CropperState`

Used by `setVisibleArea()`.

### `setBoundaryAlgorithm`

**Type:** `(state: CropperState, settings, boundary: Boundary) => CropperState`

Used when the boundary size changes, for example after a window resize.

### `transformImageAlgorithm`

**Type:** `(state: CropperState, settings, transform: ImageTransform) => CropperState`

Used by every image transform: dragging, zooming, rotating and flipping, whether by gesture or by method.

### `moveCoordinatesAlgorithm`

**Type:** `(state: CropperState, settings, directions: MoveDirections) => CropperState`

Used when the stencil is moved.

### `resizeCoordinatesAlgorithm`

**Type:** `(state: CropperState, settings, anchor: ResizeAnchor, directions: MoveDirections, options: ResizeOptions) => CropperState`

```ts
interface ResizeOptions {
	compensate?: boolean;
	preserveAspectRatio?: boolean;
	respectDirection?: 'width' | 'height';
	reference?: Coordinates | null;
}
```

Used when the stencil is resized. The default is described in [Resize Algorithm](/docs/algorithms/resize-algorithm).

<Admonition type="note" title="Not in this version">

The React docs also list `stretchAlgorithm`, `imageClassName` and `flip`/`rotate`/`zoom`/`moveImageAlgorithm` props. None of them exist in react-advanced-cropper 0.20 or in this port. Image transforms all go through `transformImageAlgorithm`, and a stretch algorithm is passed through `boundaryProps`.

</Admonition>

## Methods

Bind the component with `bind:this` to call these methods. The bound value has the `CropperRef` type. Every callback also receives this object, so you can call the methods there too.

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
	import { Cropper, type CropperRef } from 'svelte-advanced-cropper';

	let cropper: CropperRef | undefined = $state();
</script>

<Cropper bind:this={cropper} {src} onReady={(cropper) => console.log(cropper.getState())} />
<button onclick={() => cropper?.flipImage(true)}>Flip</button>
```

<Admonition type="warning" title="Only while mounted">

The methods throw if you call them before the cropper has mounted or after it has been destroyed. Inside callbacks this never happens, and `bind:this` is `undefined` until the component mounts.

</Admonition>

Several methods take an `options` object as their last argument. Each method has its own defaults, listed with it.

| Option         | Meaning                                                                                                                    |
| -------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `transitions`  | Animate the change, if `transitions` is enabled on the cropper.                                                            |
| `immediately`  | Finish the action now: run post-processing and end it, rather than treating it as one step of an ongoing gesture.          |
| `interaction`  | Treat the call as a user interaction, which sets the interaction flags and fires the interaction callbacks.                |
| `normalize`    | The values are on-screen (boundary) pixels, and are converted to image pixels, taking rotation and flips into account.     |

### `getState()`

**Returns:** `CropperState | null`

```ts
interface CropperState {
	boundary: Boundary;
	imageSize: ImageSize;
	transforms: Transforms;
	visibleArea: VisibleArea | null;
	coordinates: Coordinates | null;
}
```

The current [cropper state](/docs/concept/state), or `null` before an image has loaded. The getters read reactive state, so you can call them in a template or a `$derived` and they update by themselves.

### `getDefaultState()`

**Returns:** `CropperState | null`

The state a fresh reset would produce for the current image and boundary, or `null` while either is missing. Compare it with `getState()` to find out whether the user has changed anything.

### `getCoordinates(options)`

**Arguments:** `options?: { round?: boolean }` (`round` defaults to `true`)

**Returns:** `Coordinates | null`

```ts
interface Coordinates {
	left: number;
	top: number;
	width: number;
	height: number;
}
```

The crop area in image pixels, rounded to whole pixels unless you pass `round: false`.

<Admonition type="note" title="Coordinates of a rotated image">

When the image is rotated, the coordinates are measured in the bounding box of the rotated image (the dashed frame below), not in the original image.

<RotateCoordinatesExample />

</Admonition>

### `getVisibleArea()`

**Returns:** `VisibleArea | null`

The part of the image currently shown in the boundary, as `{ left, top, width, height }` in image pixels.

### `getStencilCoordinates()`

**Returns:** `{ left: number; top: number; width: number; height: number }`

The stencil's position and size in boundary (on-screen) pixels. Stencils use it to place themselves.

### `getTransforms()`

**Returns:** `Transforms`

```ts
interface Transforms {
	rotate: number;
	flip: {
		horizontal: boolean;
		vertical: boolean;
	};
}
```

The current rotation, in degrees, and flips of the image.

### `getTransitions()`

**Returns:** `CropperTransitions`

```ts
interface CropperTransitions {
	timingFunction: string;
	duration: number;
	active: boolean;
}
```

The transition settings, plus `active`, which is `true` while a transition runs.

### `getInteractions()`

**Returns:** `CropperInteractions`

```ts
interface CropperInteractions {
	moveCoordinates: boolean;
	resizeCoordinates: boolean;
	transformImage: {
		rotate: boolean;
		move: boolean;
		scale: boolean;
		flip: boolean;
	};
}
```

Which interactions are in progress right now. The stencils use it, for example to show the grid only while the user is dragging.

### `hasInteractions()`

**Returns:** `boolean`

`true` while any interaction is in progress.

### `getSettings()`

**Returns:** `Settings`

The settings the cropper is using: your settings merged with the defaults and with the stencil constraints. Custom settings you pass (for example `stencilSize` on `FixedCropper`) are included.

### `getImage()`

**Returns:** `CropperImage | null`

```ts
interface CropperImage {
	src: string;
	revoke: boolean;
	transforms: Transforms;
	arrayBuffer: ArrayBuffer | null;
	width: number;
	height: number;
}
```

The loaded image, or `null`.

### `getCanvas(options)`

**Arguments:** `options?: DrawOptions`

```ts
interface DrawOptions {
	width?: number;
	height?: number;
	minWidth?: number;
	maxWidth?: number;
	minHeight?: number;
	maxHeight?: number;
	maxArea?: number;
	fillColor?: string;
	imageSmoothingEnabled?: boolean;
	imageSmoothingQuality?: 'low' | 'medium' | 'high';
}
```

**Returns:** `HTMLCanvasElement | null`

Draws the cropped area onto a canvas and returns it, or returns `null` when there is nothing to draw (no image yet, or `canvas={false}`). The options resize the result and fill transparent areas. See [Getting the result](/docs/guides/recipes#getting-the-result) and [Resize the result](/docs/guides/recipes#resize-the-result).

### `isLoading()`

**Returns:** `boolean`

`true` while an image is loading.

### `isLoaded()`

**Returns:** `boolean`

`true` once an image has loaded and until it is cleared.

### `setCoordinates(transforms, options)`

**Arguments:**

- `transforms: CoordinatesTransform | CoordinatesTransform[]`
- `options?: { transitions?: boolean; immediately?: boolean }` (both default to `true`)

```ts
type CoordinatesTransform =
	| Partial<Coordinates>
	| ((state: CropperState, settings: Settings) => Partial<Coordinates> | null);
```

Sets the crop area. The result always respects the restrictions, so you may not get exactly what you asked for. Pass an object to set known values:

```ts
cropper.setCoordinates({ left: 102, top: 74, width: 320, height: 240 });
```

Pass a function to compute the values from the state. This centers the crop area:

```ts
cropper.setCoordinates(({ coordinates, imageSize }) => ({
	left: imageSize.width / 2 - coordinates.width / 2,
	top: imageSize.height / 2 - coordinates.height / 2
}));
```

Pass an array to apply several transforms in order, each seeing the state left by the one before. That matters when one step depends on the outcome of another. Resizing and centering in a single object would center on the size you asked for, which a restriction might have changed. Two steps center on the size you actually got:

```ts
cropper.setCoordinates([
	{ width: 400, height: 300 },
	({ coordinates, imageSize }) => ({
		left: imageSize.width / 2 - coordinates.width / 2,
		top: imageSize.height / 2 - coordinates.height / 2
	})
]);
```

See [Set coordinates](/docs/guides/advanced-recipes#set-coordinates).

### `setVisibleArea(visibleArea, options)`

**Arguments:**

- `visibleArea: VisibleArea`
- `options?: { transitions?: boolean; immediately?: boolean }` (both default to `true`)

Shows a different part of the image. The crop area is adjusted if it no longer fits.

### `setState(state, options)`

**Arguments:**

- `state: CropperState | null | ((state: CropperState | null, settings) => CropperState | null)`
- `options?: { transitions?: boolean; immediately?: boolean; interaction?: boolean; postprocess?: boolean }` (defaults: `transitions: true`, the rest `false`)

Replaces the whole state as given, without the checks the other methods make. Treat it as an escape hatch for what they can't do, such as restoring a saved state.

### `setImage(image)`

**Arguments:** `image: CropperImage`

Swaps the image object without resetting the state, for example to show a processed version of the same image. Normally you change `src` instead.

### `zoomImage(scale, options)`

**Arguments:**

- `scale: number | Scale`
- `options?: { transitions?: boolean; immediately?: boolean; interaction?: boolean; normalize?: boolean }` (defaults: `transitions: true`, `immediately: true`, `interaction: false`, `normalize: false`)

```ts
interface Scale {
	factor: number;
	center?: { left: number; top: number };
}
```

Zooms the image by a relative factor: `2` zooms in twice, `0.5` zooms out. The zoom is centered on the visible area unless you give a `center`. See [Move and scale image](/docs/guides/advanced-recipes#move-and-scale-image).

### `moveImage(left, top, options)`

**Arguments:**

- `left: number`
- `top?: number`
- `options?: { transitions?: boolean; immediately?: boolean; interaction?: boolean; normalize?: boolean }` (same defaults as `zoomImage`)

Moves the image by the given offsets. See [Move and scale image](/docs/guides/advanced-recipes#move-and-scale-image).

### `rotateImage(rotate, options)`

**Arguments:**

- `rotate: number | Rotate`
- `options?: { transitions?: boolean; immediately?: boolean; interaction?: boolean; normalize?: boolean }` (same defaults as `zoomImage`)

```ts
interface Rotate {
	angle: number;
	center?: { left: number; top: number };
}
```

Rotates the image by an angle in **degrees**, relative to the current rotation. See [Rotate / flip image](/docs/guides/advanced-recipes#rotate-flip-image).

### `flipImage(horizontal, vertical, options)`

**Arguments:**

- `horizontal?: boolean`
- `vertical?: boolean`
- `options?: { transitions?: boolean; immediately?: boolean; interaction?: boolean; normalize?: boolean }` (same defaults as `zoomImage`, except `normalize: true`)

Flips the image horizontally, vertically or both. With `normalize`, the axes are the on-screen ones, even when the image is rotated by 90 degrees.

### `transformImage(transform, options)`

**Arguments:**

- `transform: ImageTransform`
- `options?: { transitions?: boolean; immediately?: boolean; interaction?: boolean; normalize?: boolean }` (defaults: `transitions: true`, `immediately: false`, `interaction: true`, `normalize: true`)

```ts
interface ImageTransform {
	scale?: number | Scale;
	move?: { left?: number; top?: number };
	rotate?: number | Rotate;
	flip?: { horizontal?: boolean; vertical?: boolean };
}
```

The general image transform that the gesture layer uses. `zoomImage`, `moveImage`, `rotateImage` and `flipImage` are shortcuts for it with different defaults. By default it counts as an ongoing interaction, so finish it with `transformImageEnd()`.

### `transformImageEnd(options)`

**Arguments:** `options?: { transitions?: boolean; immediately?: boolean }` (both default to `true`)

Ends an image transform started with `transformImage()`: post-processing runs and the interaction flags are cleared.

### `moveCoordinates(directions, options)`

**Arguments:**

- `directions: Partial<MoveDirections>`, that is `{ left?: number; top?: number }`
- `options?: { transitions?: boolean; immediately?: boolean; interaction?: boolean; normalize?: boolean }` (defaults: `interaction: true`, `normalize: true`, the rest `false`)

Moves the crop area by the given offsets, in on-screen pixels by default. Stencils call it while the user drags them.

### `moveCoordinatesEnd(options)`

**Arguments:** `options?: { transitions?: boolean; immediately?: boolean }` (defaults: `transitions: true`, `immediately: false`)

Ends a move started with `moveCoordinates()`.

### `resizeCoordinates(anchor, directions, parameters, options)`

**Arguments:**

- `anchor: ResizeAnchor`: the handle being dragged (`'north'`, `'eastSouth'`, …, or `'center'`)
- `directions: Partial<MoveDirections>`: how far the handle moved
- `parameters?: ResizeOptions`: see `resizeCoordinatesAlgorithm` above
- `options?: { transitions?: boolean; immediately?: boolean; interaction?: boolean; normalize?: boolean }` (same defaults as `moveCoordinates`)

Resizes the crop area as if the given handle were dragged. Stencils call it through [`BoundingBox`](/docs/components/BoundingBox).

### `resizeCoordinatesEnd(options)`

**Arguments:** `options?: { transitions?: boolean; immediately?: boolean }` (defaults: `transitions: true`, `immediately: false`)

Ends a resize started with `resizeCoordinates()`.

### `reconcileState(options)`

**Arguments:** `options?: { transitions?: boolean }` (defaults to `false`)

Repairs the state if it no longer satisfies the settings. The cropper does this by itself unless `autoReconcileState` is off.

### `startTransitions()`

Marks transitions as active for one transition duration, firing `onTransitionsStart` and, later, `onTransitionsEnd`. Call it before a change that your own components animate (for example a custom cropper switching layouts), so that `getTransitions().active` is `true` while the animation runs.

### `refresh()`

**Returns:** `Promise<void>`

Measures the boundary again and updates the state for the new size. The cropper calls it on every window resize. Call it yourself when the container changes size without the window changing. See [Dynamic cropper size](/docs/guides/advanced-recipes#dynamic-cropper-size).

### `reset()`

**Returns:** `Promise<void>`

Measures the boundary and recreates the default state for the current image, discarding every change the user made.

### `clear()`

Removes the state, so the stencil and the image disappear until the next reset.

## Callbacks

Every callback receives the cropper (the same `CropperRef` that `bind:this` gives you) as its only argument. Callbacks only fire while the cropper is mounted.

### `onReady`

**Type:** `(cropper: CropperRef) => void`

Fires when an image has loaded and its initial state has rendered, so `getCanvas()` already works inside it.

### `onError`

**Type:** `(cropper: CropperRef) => void`

Fires when the image fails to load.

### `onChange`

**Type:** `(cropper: CropperRef) => void`

Fires whenever the state changes: the crop area, the visible area or the transforms.

### `onUpdate`

**Type:** `(cropper: CropperRef) => void`

Fires on any change at all: the state, transitions, interactions, loading and the image. Use it to keep something in sync with everything the cropper shows.

### `onTransitionsStart`

**Type:** `(cropper: CropperRef) => void`

Fires when a transition starts.

### `onTransitionsEnd`

**Type:** `(cropper: CropperRef) => void`

Fires when a transition ends.

### `onMove`

**Type:** `(cropper: CropperRef) => void`

Fires while the crop area is being moved.

### `onMoveEnd`

**Type:** `(cropper: CropperRef) => void`

Fires when moving the crop area ends.

### `onResize`

**Type:** `(cropper: CropperRef) => void`

Fires while the crop area is being resized.

### `onResizeEnd`

**Type:** `(cropper: CropperRef) => void`

Fires when resizing the crop area ends.

### `onTransformImage`

**Type:** `(cropper: CropperRef) => void`

Fires while the image is being transformed, by a mouse drag, the wheel or a touch gesture.

### `onTransformImageEnd`

**Type:** `(cropper: CropperRef) => void`

Fires when an image transform ends.

### `onInteractionStart`

**Type:** `(cropper: CropperRef) => void`

Fires when the user starts any interaction with the cropper.

### `onInteractionEnd`

**Type:** `(cropper: CropperRef) => void`

Fires when the last running interaction ends.
